WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
The traditional React Native architecture relied on a singular communication bridge between the JavaScript thread and the native device.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.000
For complex animations, this created a strict physical limitation.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.500
Every frame of motion had to be calculated in JavaScript, serialized, sent across the bridge and decoded on the native side.

4
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:27.000
When the application processes a heavy JSON payload or executes a complex state update, the JavaScript thread blocks.

5
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:35.000
The result is dropped animation frames and stuttering gestures, because layout calculations required to update the UI cannot cross the bridge in time.

6
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:42.000
The engineering solution to this bottleneck is the combination of React Native gesture handler 2 and reanimated 3.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
These libraries do not attempt to optimize the bridge.

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.500
Instead, they bypass standard React execution entirely.

9
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:57.500
To guarantee a fluid 120 frames per second, touch recognition and continuous animation updates are relocated exclusively to the native UI thread.

10
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:06.500
Moving execution to the UI thread isolates visual motion from business logic, but it requires abandoning standard React architectural assumptions.

11
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:15.500
Achieving highly responsive motion requires engineers to stop thinking in terms of React component life cycles and start designing for native view life cycles.

12
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:22.500
This diagram illustrates the primary architectural division, the JavaScript thread operating independently from the native UI thread.

13
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.500
The touch event drops directly into the UI lane.

14
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.500
It's intercepted by a worklet, calculating layout updates directly to the native view.

15
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.500
The JavaScript lane remains entirely empty.

16
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:44.500
Because the JavaScript thread is completely unblocked by the animation, it remains entirely free to handle business logic, application state, and background network requests without interrupting the visual frame rate.

17
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:52.500
The isolation is strict, meaning any requirement to synchronize animation data back to React state requires explicit manual intervention.

18
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.500
Engineers use the run on JS command as a manual toggle.

19
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:04.500
This explicitly sends a signal from the UI bound worklet back across the bridge to trigger a function on the JavaScript thread.

20
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:13.500
However, using run on JS to drive visual frame updates immediately reinstates the very bottleneck this architecture was built to avoid.

21
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.500
It should only be used to synchronize final state, like updating a database after a swipe is completed.

22
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.500
A threat safe UI relies on these strict execution boundaries.

23
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:30.500
The JavaScript thread acts as an asynchronous manager, while the UI thread functions as a synchronous autonomous worker.

24
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:37.500
We can observe the practical application of this pipeline by looking at the physics of interaction, starting with a basic, draggable card.

25
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:44.500
When a user swipes a card across the screen, the UI thread continuously calculates the vector and speed of the finger in motion.

26
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.500
This diagram demonstrates velocity handoff.

27
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:53.500
The release speed of the user's finger transfers directly into the initial state of the spring equation.

28
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.500
Without this velocity parameter, the spring calculates its start from zero.

29
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.500
The card's stutters before the spring accelerates.

30
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:08.500
Maintaining true physical accuracy relies entirely on the fact that this velocity data never waits on a JavaScript bridge to resolve.

31
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.500
For complex interfaces, gesture compositions splits logic into distinct paths.

32
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.500
Simultaneous allows gestures to recognize concurrently.

33
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.500
Exclusive dictates the first gesture blocks all others.

34
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:27.500
In a race condition, the first gesture to hit its activation threshold cancels all other potential gestures, securing sole control.

35
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:39.500
Additionally, when building nested scrolling interfaces, require external gesture to fail ensures that an inner scroll area remains inactive until the outer swipe gesture is mathematically proven to have failed.

36
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:50.500
UI fluidity is the result of continuous physics math and discrete state logic executing natively on a millisecond basis, devoid of latency.

37
00:03:50.500 --> 00:04:00.500
Beyond touch interactions, this native execution model extends to layout changes, the physical mounting, unmounting, and resizing of UI components.

38
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.500
Historically, react renders drove layout animations.

39
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:10.500
Fabric architecture shifts authority, directly commanding the view hierarchy, independent of render cycles.

40
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:19.500
Entering and exiting animation presets are now strictly triggered by Fabric's native lifecycle events, the animation runs at the native view level.

41
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:32.500
When animating items within a changing list, layout transitions, like linear transition, or sequence transition, mathematically calculate and execute the movement of sibling components as adjacent elements mount or unmount.

42
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.500
This architecture introduces a critical performance trap.

43
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:43.500
Engineers frequently instantiate reanimated presets, like fade in, directly inside an inline component map function.

44
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:53.500
Because React rebuilds the mapping on every cycle, writing the preset in line forces the engine to generate entirely new animation builder object in memory on every single render.

45
00:04:53.500 --> 00:05:02.500
The strict engineering rule to prevent this is to always define animation presets outside the component scope entirely, or properly memoize them within a used memo hook.

46
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:07.500
For complex initial renders, reanimated provides the layout animation config wrapper.

47
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:17.500
By wrapping a list in layout animation config, you can disable all layout animations for that specific subtree on mount, preventing visual chaos when rendering dozens of list items simultaneously.

48
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:26.500
Under Fabric, optimizing animations means engineers must stop attempting to animate react components and focus strictly on animating the resulting native layout nodes.

49
00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:33.500
Relying on the native view lifecycle introduces platform specific architectural friction points that conflict with standard react development patterns.

50
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:44.500
This diagram details Android view flattening. To optimize memory, Android natively removes intermediate views lacking visual styling, condensing the hierarchy.

51
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:52.500
This silently breaks exiting animations. Reanimated fails to attach the animation hook because the view has already been deleted natively.

52
00:05:52.500 --> 00:06:02.500
The required fix is explicit, applying the collapsible equals false property to the animated view forces Android to keep the empty node alive in memory, allowing the animation hook to attach.

53
00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.500
The second major friction point occurs when non-animated react parents unmount.

54
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:12.500
When a parent component unmounts and react, it ruthlessly destroys its native children instantly.

55
00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:17.500
If those children have in-progress exit animations, they are immediately terminated. The parent does not wait.

56
00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:27.500
To preserve the motion, either the parent itself must be animated to physically delay its own unmount, or the children must be portal to render entirely outside the unmounting parent's tree.

57
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:40.500
Furthermore, if a parent component explicitly overrides its native ID, it blinds reanimated's internal tracking, disabling entering animations for all descendant views unless they are wrapped in an undecorated view container.

58
00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:49.500
React's declarative assumptions regarding tree destruction will perpetually conflict with native animation life cycles. The engineer must explicitly manage the boundary between the two.

59
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.500
Under maximum load, the value of this architecture is absolute.

60
00:06:53.500 --> 00:07:01.500
The native UI thread operates in a continuous loop, capturing gestures, executing velocity handoffs, and calculating transitions framed by frame.

61
00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:07.500
Simultaneously, the isolated JavaScript lane processes massive background payloads without ever dropping a single visual frame.

62
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:15.500
In modern React native, React provides the structural blueprint and manages the state, but the actual interaction is purely native execution.

63
00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:30.500
By mastering UI workloads, physical velocity mathematics, and native view life cycles, engineers elevate their applications from hybrid approximations into true native experiences, completely immune to the limitations of JavaScript.

